#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147870 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147594 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "163d387b-3fd7-5f95-8245-5fb0184b371b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Decimal Percentage"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b445a131-81f0-5514-a192-d5eb422bef26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$112$ kilograms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144975} {:answer/id #uuid "c5e8430f-d1aa-5170-bb82-785391bf344f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$128$ kilograms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144976} {:answer/id #uuid "d8a296a4-550e-588e-b32e-e9e4afbfb734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$117$ kilograms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144977} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b6ecbc-2867-5b7a-a528-e7f2ca3a3967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$121$ kilograms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144978} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0261e5-a932-5e3f-8956-c8d434bf15ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$130$ kilograms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8a296a4-550e-588e-b32e-e9e4afbfb734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$117$ kilograms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144977} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0247569-e442-53e7-b321-6e1aaaeee8a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7988730-acb0-5c20-b331-3dbab826b8f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-61174" :question/problem "A concrete mix weighs $600$ kilograms. Of this amount, $19.5%$ represents the weight of the crushed stone in the mix. How much crushed stone is there in the concrete mix?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the weight of the concrete mix, which is $600$ kilograms. The percentage is $19.5%,$ and the part is the weight of the crushed stone in the mix.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n19.5%=19.5÷100=0.195\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =19.5%\\times 600 \\\\  & =0.195\\times 600 \\\\  & =117\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $19.5%$ of $600$ is $117.$ Thus, there are $117$ kilograms of crushed stone in the concrete mix.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n19.5%=\\frac{19.5}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =19.5%\\times 600 \\\\  & =\\frac{19.5}{100}\\times 600 \\\\  & =19.5\\times \\frac{600}{100} \\\\  & =19.5\\times 6 \\\\  & =117\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $19.5%$ of $600$ is $117.$ Thus, there are $117$ kilograms of crushed stone in the concrete mix."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491195 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147595 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "176069e4-4aa3-58cd-a04e-a70e7e5da960" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Percentage Greater Than 100%"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29b3a452-1742-57c1-9f1e-bc77b9df5dd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "420" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490589} {:answer/id #uuid "61ce839e-834f-54a5-9c9e-f00fd0753c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490693} {:answer/id #uuid "744129d7-d0ce-5c8c-98a9-76b25bb784a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "320" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490739} {:answer/id #uuid "b833ff6e-69ba-52a2-81d4-7ba41f29f665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "430" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490876} {:answer/id #uuid "e1bd0ebf-e3a4-517c-90b3-10048e44189a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "380" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61ce839e-834f-54a5-9c9e-f00fd0753c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490693} :answer-field/id #uuid "f229c353-7ba4-5955-b6b1-8b5659543db4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c38eb0c5-c781-5447-a06b-aea59b9ebae6" :question/math-academy-id "q-61182" :question/problem "On the opening day of the children's baseball season, $300$ people attended the baseball game. The next game was attended by $120%$ of the number of people who attended the opening game. How many people attended the second game?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the total number of people who attended the opening game, which is $300$ people. The percentage is $120%,$ and the part is the number of people who attended the second game.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n120%=120÷100=1.2\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =120%\\times 300 \\\\  & =1.2\\times 300 \\\\  & =360\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $120%$ of $300$ is $360.$ So, $360$ people attended the second game.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n120%=\\frac{120}{100}=\\frac{120÷20}{100÷20}=\\frac{6}{5}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =120%\\times 300 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\times 300 \\\\  & =6\\times \\frac{300}{5} \\\\  & =6\\times 60 \\\\  & =360\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $120%$ of $300$ is $360.$ So, $360$ people attended the second game."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147595 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "176069e4-4aa3-58cd-a04e-a70e7e5da960" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Percentage Greater Than 100%"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3fed532-6ecc-54f6-bad0-b6ad07df33aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$21$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144987} {:answer/id #uuid "2fbe64f4-a22c-5777-a3ec-19b32abc5173" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$22$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144988} {:answer/id #uuid "33f5d0f0-b1cd-5f13-9fb4-4005ac8072ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$24$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144989} {:answer/id #uuid "767643b6-d9d5-5d8b-94f6-d58fe32ca1f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$18$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144990} {:answer/id #uuid "7770f937-95a0-55a1-bb09-4a74b4daacc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$28$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33f5d0f0-b1cd-5f13-9fb4-4005ac8072ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$24$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144989} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b14e3b7-0f48-5b1e-a3df-3a11bc0b2ed6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "290a6299-631d-5270-a788-c595be029595" :question/math-academy-id "q-61188" :question/problem "Anna, a dressmaker, bought $15$ meters of fabric in a particular month to make children's pants. The following month, she decided to buy $160%$ of the amount of fabric she bought the previous month. How much fabric did Anna buy on her second purchase?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the amount of fabric Anna bought the first time, which is $15$ meters. The percentage is $160%,$ and the part is the amount of fabric she bought on the second purchase.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n160%=160÷100=1.6\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =160%\\times 15 \\\\  & =1.6\\times 15 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $160%$ of $15$ is $24.$ So, Anna bought $24$ meters of fabric on the second purchase.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n160%=\\frac{160}{100}=\\frac{160÷20}{100÷20}=\\frac{8}{5}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =160%\\times 15 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\times 15 \\\\  & =8\\times \\frac{15}{5} \\\\  & =8\\times 3 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $160%$ of $15$ is $24.$ So, Anna bought $24$ meters of fabric on the second purchase."}]}
